+## Review, restore and retention details
+
+Manual towers retain their intersection with the completed detection run's
+boundary. Editing or clearing map drawings does not reclassify that run.
+Selected manual towers stay visible in Find and Label mode. CSV records the
+stored membership flag; KML includes selected above-threshold manual towers in
+either mode and ML results with stored membership in Find mode (all in Label).
+Training ZIPs include selected above-threshold annotations across full tiles.
+If selected ML labels are hidden in Find mode, export warns before proceeding;
+cancel to inspect them in Label mode. Switching modes resets the confidence
+slider, so restore the intended threshold before exporting.
+
+Restore accepts current exports and the known receiving cdcai format with
+contiguous ordered tiles and one leading pseudo-result per tile, all lacking ids.
+Mixed or ambiguous ids are rejected. Limits are 1,000 ZIP members, 64 MiB per
+member, 512 MiB expanded total and 8 MiB UTF-8 metadata; ZIP64 is unsupported.
+Unknown metadata fields are discarded and provider credential query values are
+redacted. A rejected upload preserves your review; you can retry the same file.
+Each referenced tile image must exist and pass JPEG/PNG/TIFF signature/structure
+checks (50 MiB, 8,192 pixels per edge, 16 million pixels). Duplicate image or
+label/XML destinations and reserved metadata collisions are rejected before
+replacement. This validates the input structure, not its training suitability.
+Valid PNG/TIFF bytes stored under legacy `.jpg` names remain accepted unchanged.
+Original search polygons are not in the archive. Existing membership flags are
+retained, while a new manual tower after restore warns that membership is
+unconfirmed (stored as false); selected manual towers remain visible/exportable.
+
+Export/restore is not a complete or lossless backup: legacy metadata-flag
+inversion and repeated manual-addition duplication remain known limitations.
+Use one active upload/review/export at a time; retain original archives. Successful
+restore discards its consumed ZIP, but reload, session reset and hard interruption
+do not guarantee removal of all old uploads or temporary files. Follow the
+[backup-aware retention procedure](maintenance.md#retention-and-recovery);
+do not prune volumes or weaken endpoint protection as a recovery step.
